Predictive Dynamical Primitives: A Theoretical Framework with Selective Risk Control, Certified Fallback, and a Falsification Protocol for Robot Motion Generation

Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM

---

Abstract

We present a formal theoretical framework for the Predictive Dynamical Primitive (PDP), a motion generation architecture that couples a dynamical policy \pi, a calibrated probabilistic success predictor f, a gating threshold \gamma, and a certified fallback policy \pi_{\text{fallback}}. This is a theoretical and methodological contribution: no experiments, physical tests, or code training have been performed. We resolve the rejection-as-failure paradox of the original formulation—where counting rejection as failure forces p_D \le p_C, rendering the hypothesis H_{1,f}: p_D - p_C > \delta logically impossible—by adopting selective prediction theory (risk–coverage curves, AURC) and a certified fallback arm. We prove that AURC is undefined for arms lacking a confidence score function, and we replace the ill-posed AURC comparison with a matched-coverage selective-risk hypothesis. We specify \pi as a genuine dynamical policy \dot x = \Phi(x;\theta) integrated over the horizon. We give a self-contained derivation of Tango's score interval for paired proportions, prove its asymptotic coverage, and correct the quadratic coefficients. We prove finite-sample selective risk control for the Learn-then-Test gating threshold, anytime-valid coverage for the empirical-Bernstein confidence sequence, and we separate robust invariance from goal reachability for the certified fallback. We provide a complete preregistered protocol for a 2-DOF testbed with seven arms (including DMP and ProMP baselines), paired perturbations, power analysis with sensitivity over the discordance rate, and decision rules for PASS, FAIL, and INCONCLUSIVE. The philosophy is Mayo's severity principle. If FAIL: STOP. No higher-DOF systems.

Keywords: movement primitives, dynamical systems, selective prediction, conformal prediction, Learn-then-Test, Tango's score interval, confidence sequences, certified fallback, falsification protocol.

---

1. Introduction

Movement primitives (MPs) provide compact, modular representations of continuous trajectories for robot motion generation. The dynamical systems (DS) perspective defines a policy as an autonomous vector field \dot x = \Phi(x; \theta), conferring time-independence and robustness to temporal perturbations. The Predictive Dynamical Primitive (PDP) augments the DS policy with a calibrated success predictor f, a gating threshold \gamma, and a certified fallback policy \pi_{\text{fallback}}.

The original formulation contained a fatal flaw. If rejection \bot counts as failure S = 0, then for any realization \omega,

S_D(\omega) = S_C(\omega) \cdot \mathbb{1}[\hat p \ge \gamma] \le S_C(\omega),

forcing p_D \le p_C pointwise. Therefore H_{1,f}: p_D - p_C > \delta with \delta > 0 is logically impossible for overall task success. This paper resolves the paradox via selective prediction theory and a certified fallback design, and it corrects several methodological inconsistencies in the original draft.

Contributions. This paper makes the following theoretical and methodological contributions:

1. Paradox resolution (Theorem 1). We prove that rejection-as-failure forces p_D \le p_C, motivating the selective prediction framework.
2. AURC well-posedness (Theorem 2). We prove that AURC is undefined for arms without a confidence score function, and we replace the ill-posed AURC hypothesis with a matched-coverage selective-risk hypothesis.
3. DS policy specification (Definition 3). We specify \pi as a genuine dynamical policy \dot x = \Phi(x;\theta) integrated over the horizon, resolving the contradiction between the DS characterization and the trajectory-level predictor.
4. Tango interval derivation (Theorem 4). We give a self-contained derivation of Tango's score interval, prove asymptotic coverage, and correct the quadratic coefficients.
5. LTT selective risk control (Theorem 5). We prove finite-sample selective risk control for the Learn-then-Test gating threshold, with fixed-sequence testing to avoid multiplicity penalties.
6. CS anytime-validity (Theorem 6). We prove anytime-valid coverage for the empirical-Bernstein confidence sequence.
7. Certified fallback (Theorem 7). We separate robust invariance from goal reachability for the recovery set and prove both under explicit conditions.
8. Preregistered protocol. We specify a complete protocol for a 2-DOF testbed with seven arms, paired perturbations, power analysis with sensitivity over \psi, and decision rules for PASS, FAIL, and INCONCLUSIVE.

Statement on empirical content. No experiments, physical tests, or code training have been performed. This paper is a theoretical and methodological contribution. All algorithms are specified for implementation, but no code has been trained or executed. All theorems are proved. The protocol is preregistered.

Organization. Section 2 develops the mathematical background. Section 3 formalizes the PDP architecture. Section 4 develops selective risk theory. Section 5 develops calibration theory. Section 6 develops Learn-then-Test. Section 7 develops statistical inference for paired proportions. Section 8 develops confidence sequences. Section 9 develops the certified fallback. Section 10 specifies the falsification protocol. Section 11 specifies the algorithms. Section 12 addresses the philosophy of science. Section 13 concludes.

