2. Mathematical Background

2.1 Robot Dynamics

Consider a robot with configuration q \in \mathcal{Q} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^n, state x = (q, \dot q) \in \mathcal{X}, and control u \in \mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m. The dynamics are

\mathcal{I}(q)\ddot q + \mathcal{K}(q,\dot q)\dot q + \mathcal{G}(q) = \tau + \tau_{\text{pert}},
\tag{1}

where \mathcal{I}(q) is the inertia matrix, \mathcal{K}(q,\dot q)\dot q collects Coriolis and centrifugal terms, \mathcal{G}(q) is gravity, and \tau_{\text{pert}} represents perturbations.

2.2 Movement Primitives

A dynamical movement primitive encodes a policy as

\dot x = \Phi(x; \theta), \quad x \in \mathcal{X},
\tag{2}

where \Phi: \mathcal{X} \to T\mathcal{X} is a vector field parameterized by \theta. The classical DMP superimposes a linear spring-damper with a nonlinear forcing term learned from demonstrations. ProMPs represent distributions over trajectories via weight vectors w \sim \mathcal{N}(\mu_w, \Sigma_w) with basis functions \Psi(t): y(t) = \Psi(t)^\top w. Recent comparisons show that DMPs and optimal control primitives are the most efficient with respect to parameter count and reconstruction accuracy, while ProMPs offer superior smoothness and velocity consistency.

2.3 Inverse Dynamics and Computed-Torque Control

A critical specification gap in the original formulation: Eq. (2) gives \dot x, not \tau. Producing \tau requires inverse dynamics:

\tau = \mathcal{I}(q)\ddot q_{\text{des}} + \mathcal{K}(q,\dot q)\dot q_{\text{des}} + \mathcal{G}(q) + \tau_{\text{fb}},
\tag{3}

where \ddot q_{\text{des}} comes from differentiating the DS. The computed-torque controller (CTC) uses the inverse dynamics model to cancel nonlinearities and coupling effects. The conventional CTC ignores modeling errors and external disturbances, but these inevitably exist and affect stability and performance.

Resolution. The PDP adopts the following convention:

\pi outputs a desired state trajectory (q_{\text{des}}(t), \dot q_{\text{des}}(t), \ddot q_{\text{des}}(t)) obtained by integrating the DS. A CTC with nominal model (\hat{\mathcal{I}}, \hat{\mathcal{K}}, \hat{\mathcal{G}}) tracks this trajectory. The tracking error dynamics are stabilized by a PD feedback term \tau_{\text{fb}} = K_p(q_{\text{des}} - q) + K_d(\dot q_{\text{des}} - \dot q). The model uncertainty (\mathcal{I} - \hat{\mathcal{I}}, \mathcal{K} - \hat{\mathcal{K}}, \mathcal{G} - \hat{\mathcal{G}}) is treated as a bounded perturbation and included in the perturbation distribution.

This is standard for DMP-based systems. The PDP does not avoid \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{K}, \mathcal{G}; it uses a nominal model for tracking and treats model error as a perturbation.

2.4 Notation

Let \mathcal{X} be the state space, \mathcal{X}^T the space of state trajectories of length T, \mathcal{M} the task manifold, and \mathcal{C} the constraint set. Let S \in \{0,1\} denote success. Let \hat p = f(x_0, x_g, \mathcal{M}, \mathcal{C}, \tau) denote the predicted success probability for a proposed trajectory \tau \in \mathcal{X}^T.

