3. The PDP Architecture

3.1 Formal Definition

Definition 1 (Predictive Dynamical Primitive). A Predictive Dynamical Primitive is a tuple

\text{PDP} = (\pi, f, \gamma, \pi_{\text{fallback}}),
\tag{4}

where:

· \pi: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{C} \to \mathcal{X}^T is a dynamical policy defined by a vector field \dot x = \Phi(x; \theta). Given (x_0, x_g, \mathcal{M}, \mathcal{C}), the parameters \theta are selected, and the DS is integrated from x_0 over T steps to produce the desired trajectory:

\tau_{\text{des}} = \text{Integrate}(\Phi(\cdot; \theta), x_0, T).
\tag{5}

· f: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{M} \times \mathcal{C} \times \mathcal{X}^T \to [0,1] is the success predictor, evaluating the full proposed trajectory;
· \gamma \in [0,1] is the gating threshold;
· \pi_{\text{fallback}}: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{U} is a certified recovery policy executed upon rejection.

Remark 1. The DS policy is autonomous: \Phi does not depend explicitly on time. Time-independence is preserved. The trajectory \tau_{\text{des}} is obtained by integration, not by one-shot Bernstein synthesis. The Bernstein parameterization (Section 11.1) is used only to select \theta via trajectory-level optimization; the executed trajectory is the DS integral.

3.2 The Gated Policy

The gated policy is a one-shot pre-execution decision:

u = 
\begin{cases}
\text{CTC}(\pi(x_0, x_g, \mathcal{M}, \mathcal{C})), & \text{if } f(x_0, x_g, \mathcal{M}, \mathcal{C}, \pi(\cdot)) \ge \gamma, \\
\pi_{\text{fallback}}(x_0, x_g), & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
\tag{6}

Gating is not state-wise. The predictor f evaluates the full proposed trajectory before execution. The decision to execute, fallback, or reject is made once.

3.3 Outcome Categories

Definition 2 (Outcome Space). Rejection is not failure. The outcome space is

\mathcal{O} = \{\text{success}, \text{failure}, \text{fallback-success}, \text{fallback-failure}\}.
\tag{7}

Success is defined as

S = 1 \iff \text{execution} \land \|q(T)-q_g\| \le \epsilon_q \land \|\dot q(T)\| \le \epsilon_v \land E \le E_{\max} \land T \le T_{\max}.
\tag{8}

The fallback arm executes \pi_{\text{fallback}}; its success is scored by the same criterion. Because \pi_{\text{fallback}} is certified to reach the recovery set \mathcal{R} and \mathcal{R} is defined to include states from which the goal is reachable, fallback-success is possible by construction.

3.4 The Rejection-as-Failure Paradox

Theorem 1 (Rejection-as-Failure Paradox). Suppose rejection \bot counts as failure, so that for every realization \omega,

S_D(\omega) = S_C(\omega) \cdot \mathbb{1}[f(\omega) \ge \gamma].
\tag{9}

Then S_D(\omega) \le S_C(\omega) for all \omega, and consequently p_D \le p_C. Therefore the hypothesis H_1: p_D - p_C > \delta with \delta > 0 is logically impossible.

Proof. Since S_C(\omega) \in \{0,1\} and \mathbb{1}[f(\omega) \ge \gamma] \in \{0,1\}, the product S_C(\omega) \cdot \mathbb{1}[f(\omega) \ge \gamma] is either 0 or S_C(\omega). In both cases, S_D(\omega) \le S_C(\omega). Taking expectations over \omega,

p_D = \mathbb{E}[S_D] \le \mathbb{E}[S_C] = p_C.

Hence p_D - p_C \le 0 always. If \delta > 0, the event p_D - p_C > \delta is impossible. \square

Remark 2. Theorem 1 is the motivation for the selective prediction framework. The resolution is twofold: (i) replace rejection-as-failure with selective prediction metrics, where abstention is its own outcome; (ii) introduce a certified fallback arm that makes abstention operationally meaningful.

