4. Selective Risk Theory

4.1 Risk–Coverage Curves

Definition 3 (Coverage and Selective Risk). Let f: \mathcal{X} \to [0,1] be a confidence score function (CSF). For a threshold \gamma \in [0,1], define the acceptance region \mathcal{A}_\gamma = \{x : f(x) \ge \gamma\}. The coverage is

c(\gamma) = \Pr[f(X) \ge \gamma],
\tag{10}

and the selective risk is

R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma) = \mathbb{E}[\text{error} \mid f(X) \ge \gamma] = \frac{\mathbb{E}[\text{error} \cdot \mathbb{1}[f(X) \ge \gamma]]}{\Pr[f(X) \ge \gamma]}.
\tag{11}

The risk–coverage curve is the set \{(c(\gamma), R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma)) : \gamma \in [0,1]\}.

4.2 Area Under the Risk–Coverage Curve

Definition 4 (AURC). The Area Under the Risk–Coverage Curve is

\text{AURC} = \int_0^1 R_{\text{sel}}(c) \, dc,
\tag{12}

where R_{\text{sel}}(c) is the selective risk at coverage c.

4.3 AURC Requires a CSF

Theorem 2 (AURC Well-Posedness). AURC is defined only for arms that possess a confidence score function. An arm without a CSF cannot produce a risk–coverage curve, and therefore cannot have an AURC.

Proof. The risk–coverage curve requires R_{\text{sel}}(c) for all c \in [0,1]. Coverage is determined by a threshold on a CSF: c(\gamma) = \Pr[f(X) \ge \gamma]. Without a CSF, the only achievable coverages are c = 0 (reject all) and c = 1 (accept all). The integral in Eq. (12) requires R_{\text{sel}}(c) for all intermediate c, which is undefined. Therefore AURC is undefined for arms without a CSF. \square

Corollary 1. In the PDP protocol, the AURC comparison is well-posed only between arms B and D (the arms with predictors). Arm C (the DS policy without a predictor) cannot have an AURC.

4.4 Matched-Coverage Selective Risk

Definition 5 (Matched-Coverage Selective Risk). Let c^* be the coverage achieved by arm D. The matched-coverage selective risk of arm C at c^* is

R_{\text{sel}}^C(c^*) = \mathbb{E}[\text{error} \mid \text{accept all}],
\tag{13}

which is the overall error rate of arm C. This is a conservative proxy for the selective risk that arm C would achieve if it possessed a CSF. It is a lower bound on the selective risk that would be obtainable with a CSF, because arm C cannot abstain and must accept all trials.

Remark 3. The matched-coverage comparison is well-defined and conservative. It isolates the value of the predictor f and the gating mechanism, without requiring arm C to produce a risk–coverage curve.

4.5 Corrected f-Ablation Hypothesis

Hypothesis 1 (f-Ablation, Corrected). Let c^* be the coverage achieved by arm D. The f-ablation hypothesis is

H_{1,f}: R_{\text{sel}}^D(c^*) < R_{\text{sel}}^C(c^*) - \delta_{\text{sel}},
\tag{14}

where \delta_{\text{sel}} > 0 is the preregistered selective-risk margin.

Remark 4. This replaces the ill-posed hypothesis H_{1,f}: \text{AURC}_D < \text{AURC}_C - \delta_{\text{AURC}} from the original draft. The corrected hypothesis is well-posed, conservative, and scientifically meaningful: it tests whether the predictor f improves selective risk at the coverage achieved by the full PDP.

4.6 Finite-Sample Estimation of AURC

Proposition 1 (AURC Estimation). Let \{(f_i, S_i)\}_{i=1}^N be a finite sample. The empirical AURC is

\widehat{\text{AURC}} = \sum_{k=1}^N \frac{1}{N} \cdot \frac{\sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{1}[S_{(i)} = 0]}{k},
\tag{15}

where (f_{(1)}, S_{(1)}), \ldots, (f_{(N)}, S_{(N)}) are sorted by decreasing f. The empirical AURC is a biased estimator of the population AURC. A bootstrap confidence interval is obtained by resampling with replacement and recomputing Eq. (15).

Remark 5. The protocol reports AURC with bootstrap confidence intervals, not as a point estimate.

