5. Calibration Theory

5.1 Proper Scoring Rules

Definition 6 (Brier Score). For N predictions \hat p_i and outcomes S_i,

\text{BS} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N (\hat p_i - S_i)^2.
\tag{16}

Definition 7 (Brier Skill Score). The Brier Skill Score normalizes by a base-rate predictor:

\text{BSS} = 1 - \frac{\text{BS}}{\text{BS}_{\text{ref}}}, \quad \text{BS}_{\text{ref}} = \bar S(1-\bar S).
\tag{17}

Definition 8 (Expected Calibration Error). For B bins,

\text{ECE} = \sum_{b=1}^B \frac{|B_b|}{N} \left| \text{acc}(B_b) - \text{conf}(B_b) \right|.
\tag{18}

5.2 Recalibration

For binary outcomes, the recalibration map is

R(p) = \mathbb{P}(S=1 \mid f = p),
\tag{19}

estimated via isotonic regression or Platt scaling on a held-out calibration set. The recalibrated predictor is \hat p_{\text{cal}} = R(\hat p). Calibration must be validated on data disjoint from training and from the policy evaluation test set.

Algorithm 1 (Platt Scaling on the Logit). Given a trained logistic model with logit z = \beta^\top \phi(x) and calibration data \{(z_i, S_i)\}_{i=1}^M:

1. Fit a, b by minimizing the negative log-likelihood

\mathcal{L}(a,b) = -\sum_{i=1}^M \left[ S_i \log \sigma(a z_i + b) + (1-S_i) \log(1 - \sigma(a z_i + b)) \right].
\tag{20}

2. Return \hat p_{\text{cal}}(x) = \sigma(a z + b).

5.3 Conformal Prediction Under Exchangeability

Definition 9 (Conformal Prediction Set). For binary classification, the prediction set is

C_\alpha(X) = \{y \in \{0,1\} : \hat p_y(X) \ge \hat q\},
\tag{21}

where \hat q is the conformal quantile of nonconformity scores on a calibration set.

Theorem 3 (Marginal Coverage). Under exchangeability of calibration and test data,

\mathbb{P}(Y \in C_\alpha(X)) \ge 1 - \alpha.
\tag{22}

Proof. Standard split conformal argument. See Vovk et al. (2005). \square

5.4 Distribution Shift

Remark 6. In robotics, sim-to-real transfer breaks exchangeability if the real distribution contains modes absent from simulation. Standard conformal formulations rely on exchangeability assumptions that are violated by the distribution shifts inherent in real-world robotics. Existing online CP methods maintain target coverage by adaptively scaling the conformal threshold, but typically employ a static nonconformity score function. Recent work such as AdaptNC proposes joint online adaptation of both the nonconformity score parameters and the conformal threshold to reduce prediction region volume while maintaining target coverage.

Resolution. In Stage 2 (hardware), we collect a real-world calibration set and apply a fresh LTT + conformal procedure. If coverage fails, the predictor is treated as failed.

