6. Learn-then-Test for Threshold Selection

6.1 Selective Risk as a Function of the Threshold

For each candidate threshold \gamma \in \Lambda = \{\gamma_1, \ldots, \gamma_K\}, the selective risk is

R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma) = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{1}[\hat p_i \ge \gamma] \cdot \mathbb{1}[S_i = 0]}{\sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{1}[\hat p_i \ge \gamma]}.
\tag{23}

6.2 The LTT Procedure

Definition 10 (LTT Threshold). LTT selects the smallest threshold satisfying the risk bound:

\hat\gamma = \min\{\gamma \in \Lambda : \text{UCB}(R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma)) \le \alpha\},
\tag{24}

where \text{UCB} is an upper confidence bound on the selective risk.

6.3 Finite-Sample Selective Risk Control

Theorem 4 (LTT Selective Risk Control). Let \Lambda = \{\gamma_1, \ldots, \gamma_K\} be candidate thresholds, and let \delta \in (0,1) be the confidence level. For each \gamma, let \text{UCB}_\gamma be an upper confidence bound on R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma) at level 1 - \delta/K. Define

\hat\gamma = \min\{\gamma \in \Lambda : \text{UCB}_\gamma \le \alpha\},

with the convention that \hat\gamma = 1 + \epsilon (sentinel: reject-all) if no such \gamma exists. Then

\mathbb{P}\left( R_{\text{sel}}(\hat\gamma) \le \alpha \right) \ge 1 - \delta.
\tag{25}

Proof. For each \gamma \in \Lambda, the event \{\text{UCB}_\gamma < R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma)\} has probability at most \delta/K by the validity of the upper confidence bound. By the union bound over K hypotheses,

\mathbb{P}\left( \exists \gamma \in \Lambda : \text{UCB}_\gamma < R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma) \right) \le K \cdot \frac{\delta}{K} = \delta.

Therefore, with probability at least 1 - \delta, we have \text{UCB}_\gamma \ge R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma) for all \gamma \in \Lambda. On this event, if \hat\gamma \in \Lambda is selected, then

R_{\text{sel}}(\hat\gamma) \le \text{UCB}_{\hat\gamma} \le \alpha.

If the sentinel is selected, the risk bound is vacuous (coverage is zero), and the protocol treats this as a predictor failure. \square

6.4 Fixed-Sequence Testing

Remark 7. Calculating many p-values would incur a multiple testing penalty, but this can be avoided using fixed-sequence testing. In fixed-sequence testing, the hypotheses are tested in a prespecified order, and testing stops at the first non-rejection. The sentinel \gamma = 1 + \epsilon is tested last. This avoids the multiplicity penalty while maintaining the finite-sample guarantee.

Corollary 2 (Fixed-Sequence LTT). Under fixed-sequence testing with the sentinel tested last, the guarantee of Theorem 4 holds with \delta/K replaced by \delta.

6.5 The Bentkus Bound

Definition 11 (Bentkus Upper Confidence Bound). For k failures in n accepted trials, the Bentkus upper confidence bound on the selective risk at level 1 - \delta is

\text{UCB}_{\text{Bentkus}}(k, n, \delta) = \sup\left\{ p \in [0,1] : \mathbb{P}(\text{Bin}(n, p) \le k) \le \delta \right\}.
\tag{26}

The Bentkus bound is tighter than the Chernoff bound for small n and is the bound used in the LTT implementation.

