7. Statistical Inference for Paired Proportions

7.1 Paired Binary Outcomes

For paired experiments where each perturbation \omega_i is applied to both baseline A and PDP D, the discordance probabilities are

p_{10} = \mathbb{P}(A=1, D=0), \quad p_{01} = \mathbb{P}(A=0, D=1).
\tag{27}

The discordance rate is \psi = p_{01} + p_{10}, and the effect is \theta = p_{01} - p_{10}.

7.2 Why Not McNemar

McNemar's test evaluates H_0: p_{01} = p_{10}—a zero effect. The PDP PASS rule tests a margin \delta > 0. Using McNemar with a margin collapses power to \alpha at the true effect \delta. Instead, we use Tango's score interval for the paired difference of proportions, which inverts a score test statistic for \theta = p_{01} - p_{10} with correct coverage properties.

7.3 Derivation of Tango's Score Interval

Setup. Let n_{11}, n_{10}, n_{01}, n_{00} be the cell counts, N = n_{11} + n_{10} + n_{01} + n_{00}. The cell probabilities are p_{11}, p_{10}, p_{01}, p_{00}, with \sum p_{ij} = 1. The parameter of interest is \theta = p_{01} - p_{10}.

Restricted MLE. Under H_0: \theta = \theta_0, the likelihood is

L(p_{11}, p_{10}, p_{01}, p_{00}) = p_{11}^{n_{11}} p_{10}^{n_{10}} p_{01}^{n_{01}} p_{00}^{n_{00}},
\tag{28}

subject to p_{01} - p_{10} = \theta_0 and \sum p_{ij} = 1.

Substituting p_{10} = p_{01} - \theta_0 and p_{11} = 1 - 2p_{01} + \theta_0 - p_{00}, the profile likelihood in p_{01} yields the restricted MLE \tilde p_{01} as the positive root of the quadratic

A \tilde p_{01}^2 + B \tilde p_{01} + C = 0,
\tag{29}

with coefficients

A = 2N, \quad B = -\left[ n_{10} + n_{01} + \theta_0(2N - n_{10} + n_{01}) \right], \quad C = n_{01}\theta_0(1+\theta_0).
\tag{30}

The positive root is

\tilde p_{01} = \frac{-B + \sqrt{B^2 - 4AC}}{2A}, \quad \tilde p_{10} = \tilde p_{01} - \theta_0.
\tag{31}

Restricted Variance. The variance of \hat\theta = \hat p_{01} - \hat p_{10} under the null is

V(\theta_0) = \frac{2\tilde p_{01} - \theta_0(1+\theta_0)}{N}.
\tag{32}

Score Statistic. The score statistic is

Z(\theta_0) = \frac{\hat\theta - \theta_0}{\sqrt{V(\theta_0)}}.
\tag{33}

Confidence Interval. The (1-\alpha) confidence interval for \theta is the set of \theta_0 for which |Z(\theta_0)| \le z_{\alpha/2}:

[\theta_L, \theta_U] = \{\theta_0 : |Z(\theta_0)| \le z_{\alpha/2}\}.
\tag{34}

The bounds are the roots of Z(\theta_0) = \pm z_{\alpha/2}, clipped at \pm 1.

7.4 Asymptotic Coverage

Theorem 5 (Tango Interval Coverage). The Tango score interval satisfies

\mathbb{P}(\theta \in [\theta_L, \theta_U]) \to 1 - \alpha \quad \text{as } N \to \infty.
\tag{35}

Proof. Under the null H_0: \theta = \theta_0, the score statistic Z(\theta_0) converges in distribution to \mathcal{N}(0,1) by the standard asymptotic theory of score tests for multinomial models. The interval is the set of \theta_0 for which |Z(\theta_0)| \le z_{\alpha/2}. By the duality between hypothesis tests and confidence intervals, the coverage probability converges to 1 - \alpha. \square

7.5 One-Sided Inference

For a one-sided PASS rule at level \alpha, we use the one-sided bound

\theta_L^{1-\alpha} = \inf\{\theta_0 : Z(\theta_0) \le z_\alpha\}.
\tag{36}

Correction. A 95% one-sided bound requires \alpha = 0.05, not \alpha = 0.10. The original draft conflated \alpha = 0.10 with a 95% one-sided bound. Throughout this paper, we use \alpha = 0.05 for the primary analysis, giving 95% one-sided bounds.

7.6 Power Analysis

Power depends on \psi and the assumed true effect \delta^*, not on \delta alone. For a one-sided test of H_0: \theta \le \delta versus H_1: \theta > \delta,

N \approx \frac{\left( z_{\alpha}\sqrt{\psi} + z_\beta \sqrt{\psi - (\delta^* - \delta)^2} \right)^2}{(\delta^* - \delta)^2}.
\tag{37}

Correction. The discordance rate \psi = p_{01} + p_{10} can range up to 1. The claim that \psi = 0.50 is conservative is false. When no pilot is available, the conservative choice is \psi = 1, not \psi = 0.50. A preregistered sensitivity analysis over \psi \in [0.2, 1.0] is required.

