8. Confidence Sequences for Anytime-Valid Inference

8.1 Optional Stopping Problem

The INCONCLUSIVE \to "add samples" rule inflates Type I error unless a maximum sample size N_{\max} and \alpha-spending function are preregistered. Confidence sequences are anytime-valid analogues of confidence intervals that do not suffer from multiplicity issues under optional continuation of the data collection.

8.2 Definition

Definition 12 (Confidence Sequence). A (1-\alpha) confidence sequence C_t satisfies

\mathbb{P}(\mu \in C_t \text{ for all } t \in \mathbb{N}) \ge 1 - \alpha.
\tag{38}

This allows repeated calculation and updating without violating coverage.

8.3 Empirical-Bernstein Confidence Sequence

Definition 13 (Empirical-Bernstein CS). For bounded observations X_1, X_2, \ldots \in [0, B], the empirical-Bernstein confidence sequence of Waudby-Smith and Ramdas is

\mathcal{C}_t = \left[ \hat\mu_t \pm \left( \sqrt{\frac{2\hat\sigma_t^2 \log(\log(2t)/\alpha)}{t}} + \frac{7B\log(\log(2t)/\alpha)}{3(t-1)} \right) \right],
\tag{39}

where \hat\mu_t is the running mean and \hat\sigma_t^2 is the running variance. The boundedness assumption X_i \in [0, B] is stated explicitly. For binary success indicators, B = 1 is natural.

Theorem 6 (Anytime-Validity). The empirical-Bernstein confidence sequence satisfies

\mathbb{P}(\mu \in \mathcal{C}_t \text{ for all } t \ge 1) \ge 1 - \alpha.
\tag{40}

Proof. The proof follows from Ville's inequality applied to the nonnegative supermartingale construction in Waudby-Smith & Ramdas (2023). The sequence is a nonnegative supermartingale under the null, and Ville's inequality bounds the probability that the supermartingale ever exceeds 1/\alpha. \square

8.4 Sequential Monitoring

Remark 8. The preregistered analysis plan specifies that Tango's interval is used for the primary fixed-sample analysis, while the confidence sequence is used for sequential monitoring. If the CS crosses the decision boundary before N_{\max}, the trial stops early and the decision is reported as PASS or FAIL accordingly. If the CS does not cross by N_{\max}, the outcome is INCONCLUSIVE.

8.5 Choice Between Tango and the CS

Proposition 2. The fixed-sample Tango interval has higher power than the CS at a fixed sample size, because the CS is conservative to maintain anytime-validity. The CS is used only for sequential monitoring.

Proof. The CS radius in Eq. (39) includes the \log(\log(2t)/\alpha) term, which is larger than the fixed-sample z_{\alpha/2} term for all finite t. Therefore the CS is wider than the fixed-sample interval at the same sample size. \square

