10. Falsification Protocol

10.1 System Specification

System. Actuated double pendulum. Inertia matrix:

\mathcal{I}(q) = \begin{pmatrix} m_1 l_1^2 + m_2(l_1^2 + l_2^2 + 2l_1l_2\cos q_2) & m_2(l_2^2 + l_1l_2\cos q_2) \\ m_2(l_2^2 + l_1l_2\cos q_2) & m_2 l_2^2 \end{pmatrix},
\tag{42}

with \mathcal{K}(q,\dot q) and \mathcal{G}(q) derived accordingly. Nominal parameters: m_1 = m_2 = 1 kg, l_1 = l_2 = 0.5 m, g = 9.81 m/s².

Torque feasibility. For a vertical plane with point masses, the joint-1 gravity torque at the horizontal pose is approximately 14.7 N·m, above the 10 N·m limit. The goal distribution q_g is therefore restricted to states where the required torque does not exceed the limit. A saturation and anti-windup controller is specified for the CTC. The preregistered goal distribution is the set of reachable states with gravity torque below 8 N·m.

Task. Point-to-point q_0 \to q_g with T_{\max} = 2 s, |\tau_i| \le 10 N·m, E \le 50 J, \epsilon_q = 0.05 rad, \epsilon_v = 0.1 rad/s.

Perturbations. Preregistered distributions are applied in software, not via physical mass changes:

\hat m_i \sim \mathcal{U}[0.9 m_i, 1.1 m_i], \quad \hat\mu \sim \mathcal{U}[0.05, 0.15], \quad q_0 \sim \mathcal{N}(\bar q_0, 0.01 I),

\tau_{\text{noise}} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, 0.1^2), \quad d_{\text{delay}} \sim \mathcal{U}[0, 5] \text{ ms}, \quad \text{model error} \sim \mathcal{U}[-0.1, 0.1] \text{ on } \mathcal{I}, \mathcal{K}, \mathcal{G}.
\tag{43}

Unmodeled hardware effects. Backlash, gearbox friction, reflected rotor inertia (6:1 gives 36×), and current-loop lag are systematic, not within \pm 10\%. A preregistered system-identification step is performed before the experimental trials. The identified parameters are used to set the nominal model and to define the model-error perturbation distribution.

10.2 Experimental Arms

Seven arms are compared:

Arm Policy f Gating Fallback Trigger
A iLQR + LQR No No No —
A′ iLQR + LQR No No Certified recovery Oracle trigger
B iLQR + LQR Yes Yes Certified recovery Predictor gate
C \pi (DS policy) No No No —
D \pi (DS policy) Yes Yes Certified recovery Predictor gate
E DMP + CTC No No No —
F ProMP + CTC No No No —

Arm A′ specification. Arm A′ uses an oracle trigger: the fallback is triggered if the true perturbation exceeds a threshold. This is an upper bound on the benefit of the fallback without a predictor. If the oracle trigger is not implementable, A′ is removed and the fallback is evaluated only in the full PDP (arms B and D).

Same perturbation realizations for all arms. Order randomized in permuted blocks. A sealed test seed is used, with separate dev seeds for tuning.

Controller confound. All arms use the same CTC with the same K_p, K_d gains. The tracking controller is not varied across arms. The comparison isolates the high-level policy and the predictor, not the low-level controller.

Equal tuning budget. All arms receive equal tuning budget (same number of hyperparameter evaluations, same compute time).

DMP and ProMP tuning. DMPs can learn from a single demonstration; ProMPs require multiple demonstrations to achieve competitive performance. The protocol specifies the number of demonstrations used for each baseline and whether the tuning budget includes the demonstration collection.

10.3 Ablation Structure

· D vs A: overall success probability. Primary metric.
· D vs B: overall success probability. Isolates \pi (both arms contain f, gating, fallback).
· D vs C: matched-coverage selective risk and AURC. Isolates f and gating. Overall success is reported as secondary and is not the primary test.
· D vs E: overall success probability. Compares PDP against DMP baseline.
· D vs F: overall success probability. Compares PDP against ProMP baseline.

10.4 Primary Hypotheses

Primary hypothesis:

H_1: p_D - p_A > \delta, \quad p_D > 0.5.
\tag{44}

Ablation hypotheses:

H_{1,\pi}: p_D - p_B > \delta, \quad H_{1,f}: R_{\text{sel}}^D(c^*) < R_{\text{sel}}^C(c^*) - \delta_{\text{sel}}.
\tag{45}

10.5 Decision Rules

Using Tango's one-sided interval [\theta_L, \theta_U] for p_D - p_A at \alpha = 0.05:

\text{PASS} \iff \theta_L > \delta \;\land\; \text{LCB}_{95\%}(p_D) > 0.5.
\tag{46}

\text{FAIL} \iff \theta_U < \delta \;\lor\; \text{UCB}_{95\%}(p_D) < 0.5.
\tag{47}

Otherwise, INCONCLUSIVE. Inconclusive outcomes trigger a preregistered sample size increase up to N_{\max}, not a reinterpretation.

10.6 Predictor Validation

The predictor f passes if

\text{ECE} \le 0.05, \quad \text{Brier} \le 0.15, \quad \text{BSS} \ge 0.20, \quad \text{AUROC} \ge 0.75.
\tag{48}

These values are preregistered. If the predictor fails, the arms B and D are uninterpretable, and the primary comparison is declared INCONCLUSIVE for the full architecture. The C vs A comparison is reported as secondary/exploratory. Replacing the primary hypothesis with C vs A violates the preregistration principle.

10.7 Two-Stage Protocol

Stage 1 (simulation). Full protocol, including calibration and gating. If FAIL in simulation, STOP. No hardware.

Stage 2 (hardware). Only if Stage 1 PASSes. Re-preregister the hardware distribution; sim-to-real transfer breaks exchangeability. Collect a real-world calibration set and apply a fresh LTT + conformal procedure. Report results separately.

10.8 Falsification Rule V0.4

Falsification Rule V0.4. The PDP instantiation (\pi, f, \gamma, \pi_{\text{fallback}}) is rejected if, on the preregistered test distribution, either:

(i) the upper confidence bound for p_D - p_A is below \delta, or

(ii) the upper confidence bound for p_D is below 0.5.

If neither condition holds and the lower bounds do not exceed the thresholds, the outcome is INCONCLUSIVE. If INCONCLUSIVE at N_{\max}, the instantiation is not rejected but is also not supported; no further sampling is performed. No post hoc modification of metrics, thresholds, baseline, distribution, or architecture is permitted. Negative results are published. Code and data are preserved. If FAIL: STOP. No transition to higher-DOF systems.

10.9 Multiplicity Control

For three primary comparisons (D-A, D-B, D-C), control the family-wise error rate. The Holm–Bonferroni procedure is preferred over Bonferroni for greater power:

1. Order p-values: p_{(1)} \le p_{(2)} \le p_{(3)}.
2. Reject H_{(k)} if p_{(k)} \le \alpha/(m-k+1) for all j \le k.

The hypothesis p_D > 0.5 is included in the Holm family. Do not claim FDR control unless Benjamini–Hochberg is used. For three comparisons, FWER control is appropriate. With five primary comparisons (D-A, D-B, D-C, D-E, D-F), either keep only the three core comparisons in the primary family and treat D-E / D-F as secondary, or apply Holm to all five and accept the power loss. The choice is documented before data collection.

