12. Philosophy of Science Foundations

12.1 The Duhem–Quine Underdetermination Thesis

The hypothesis "\exists a compact representation that works for some task class" is existential. A single failed instantiation does not falsify it—a different \pi, different \theta, or different testbed might succeed. Duhem argued that one can never conclusively falsify a single hypothesis; we test hypotheses only in combination with auxiliary assumptions. When an experiment fails, we can always blame an auxiliary.

Reframing. The claim is about this instantiation: "The proposed (\pi, f, \gamma, \pi_{\text{fallback}}) with the specified \theta dimension and task class achieves p_D > 0.5 and beats baseline by \delta." The task class is operationally defined before the experiment:

· Dimension: 2-DOF;
· Smoothness: C^2 desired trajectories;
· Constraint structure: box constraints on torque and energy;
· Time-horizon ratio: T_{\max} / T_{\text{natural}} \in [0.8, 1.2].

The auxiliary assumptions include:

· The perturbation distribution and its stationarity;
· The inverse dynamics model and its accuracy;
· The fallback policy and its recovery set;
· The predictor's calibration set and its exchangeability with the test distribution.

The falsification rule can only be severe if these auxiliaries are preregistered and not adjusted after seeing the data.

12.2 Popper, Mayo, and Lakatos

Popper: The hypothesis must be falsifiable. The point null is maximally falsifiable because it commits to a single value.

Mayo: The test must be severe. Data x are evidence for H only to the extent that H passes a test that probably would have found flaws in H, if they existed. Severity is the basis for constructing rival theories which existing tests cannot distinguish.

Lakatos: Theories are tested in research programmes, with auxiliary hypotheses. A failed instantiation may not kill the programme if the auxiliary assumptions are independently testable. Preregister which auxiliaries are adjustable and which are not.

12.3 Methodological Discipline

The preregistered protocol, sealed test seeds, and automated orchestration are the operational expression of methodological discipline. The changelog tone of earlier drafts is replaced by a neutral, academic register. No claims of "certification" are made unless a formal invariance proof or numerical verification is provided.

