Appendix A: Mathematical Foundations of the PDP Framework

A.1 Notation and Standing Assumptions

Throughout the appendices, we adopt the following notation and assumptions.

Assumption A.1 (Bounded State and Control). The state space \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^{2n} is compact, and the control space \mathcal{U} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^m is compact. The dynamics f: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{U} \times \mathcal{D} \to \mathcal{X} are continuous, where \mathcal{D} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d is the compact disturbance set.

Assumption A.2 (Bounded Disturbances). For all d \in \mathcal{D}, \|d\| \le \bar d < \infty.

Assumption A.3 (Lipschitz Dynamics). The dynamics f are Lipschitz continuous in (x, u) with constants L_x, L_u:

\|f(x, u, d) - f(x', u', d)\| \le L_x \|x - x'\| + L_u \|u - u'\|.

Assumption A.4 (Bounded Observations). All random variables X_1, X_2, \ldots used in the confidence sequence construction satisfy X_i \in [0, B] for some known B < \infty. For binary success indicators, B = 1.

Assumption A.5 (Exchangeability). In the conformal prediction analysis, calibration and test data are exchangeable. In the sim-to-real setting, this assumption is explicitly acknowledged as violated, and the protocol specifies a fresh calibration procedure.

Assumption A.6 (Stationarity of the Test Distribution). The preregistered test distribution P_{\text{test}} is stationary during the data collection period. No distributional drift is assumed within Stage 1 or Stage 2.

A.2 Dynamics and Trajectory Generation

The robot dynamics are given by

\mathcal{I}(q)\ddot q + \mathcal{K}(q,\dot q)\dot q + \mathcal{G}(q) = \tau + \tau_{\text{pert}}.
\tag{A.1}

The DS policy \dot x = \Phi(x; \theta) is autonomous and Lipschitz in x. The trajectory is obtained by numerical integration:

x_{k+1} = x_k + \Delta t \cdot \Phi(x_k; \theta), \quad k = 0, 1, \ldots, T-1,
\tag{A.2}

with x_0 given and \Delta t = 1 ms. The desired acceleration \ddot q_{\text{des}} is obtained by finite differences of \dot q_{\text{des}}.

The computed-torque controller (CTC) with nominal model (\hat{\mathcal{I}}, \hat{\mathcal{K}}, \hat{\mathcal{G}}) computes

\tau = \hat{\mathcal{I}}(q)\ddot q_{\text{des}} + \hat{\mathcal{K}}(q,\dot q)\dot q_{\text{des}} + \hat{\mathcal{G}}(q) + K_p(q_{\text{des}} - q) + K_d(\dot q_{\text{des}} - \dot q).
\tag{A.3}

The tracking error dynamics under model uncertainty are treated as a bounded perturbation:

\Delta \mathcal{I}(q)\ddot q + \Delta \mathcal{K}(q,\dot q)\dot q + \Delta \mathcal{G}(q) = \tau_{\text{pert}},
\tag{A.4}

where \Delta \mathcal{I} = \mathcal{I} - \hat{\mathcal{I}}, etc. The perturbation set \mathcal{D} is defined by the bounds on (\Delta \mathcal{I}, \Delta \mathcal{K}, \Delta \mathcal{G}).

A.3 Selective Prediction Theory: Detailed Derivations

A.3.1 Risk–Coverage Curve

Let f: \mathcal{X} \to [0,1] be a confidence score function (CSF). For a threshold \gamma \in [0,1], the acceptance region is \mathcal{A}_\gamma = \{x : f(x) \ge \gamma\}. The coverage is

c(\gamma) = \Pr[f(X) \ge \gamma] = \int \mathbb{1}[f(x) \ge \gamma] \, dP(x).
\tag{A.5}

The selective risk is

R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma) = \mathbb{E}[\ell(f(X), Y) \mid f(X) \ge \gamma] = \frac{\mathbb{E}[\ell(f(X), Y) \cdot \mathbb{1}[f(X) \ge \gamma]]}{\Pr[f(X) \ge \gamma]}.
\tag{A.6}

For binary outcomes with 0–1 loss \ell(\hat y, y) = \mathbb{1}[\hat y \neq y], this becomes

R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma) = \frac{\mathbb{E}[\mathbb{1}[S=0] \cdot \mathbb{1}[f(X) \ge \gamma]]}{\Pr[f(X) \ge \gamma]}.
\tag{A.7}

The AURC is

\text{AURC} = \int_0^1 R_{\text{sel}}(c) \, dc,
\tag{A.8}

where R_{\text{sel}}(c) is the selective risk at coverage c. An equivalent expression using the CSF is

\text{AURC} = \int_0^1 \int \frac{\mathbb{E}[\ell(f(x), y) \mathbb{1}[f(x) \ge f(\hat x)]]}{\Pr[f(x') \ge f(\hat x)]} \, dP(x,y) \, dP(\hat x).
\tag{A.9}

A.3.2 Finite-Sample AURC Estimation

For a finite sample \{(f_i, S_i)\}_{i=1}^N, sort the samples by decreasing f: (f_{(1)}, S_{(1)}), \ldots, (f_{(N)}, S_{(N)}). The empirical AURC is

\widehat{\text{AURC}} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{k=1}^N \frac{1}{k} \sum_{i=1}^k \mathbb{1}[S_{(i)} = 0].
\tag{A.10}

This estimator is biased. The bias is O(1/N) under regularity conditions. A bootstrap confidence interval is constructed by resampling N indices with replacement B = 10{,}000 times and computing the 2.5\% and 97.5\% quantiles of the bootstrap AURC distribution.

Algorithm A.1 (Bootstrap AURC Confidence Interval).

```
Input: predictions {(f_i, S_i)}_{i=1}^N, number of bootstrap samples B, level α
Output: confidence interval [AURC_L, AURC_U]

1. Compute AURC_hat via Eq. (A.10).
2. For b = 1, ..., B:
   a. Sample N indices with replacement from {1, ..., N}.
   b. Compute AURC_hat_b on the bootstrap sample.
3. Sort {AURC_hat_b}_{b=1}^B.
4. Set AURC_L = quantile(α/2), AURC_U = quantile(1 - α/2).
5. Return [AURC_L, AURC_U].
```

A.3.3 Matched-Coverage Selective Risk

Let c^* be the coverage achieved by arm D:

c^* = \Pr[f_D(X) \ge \hat\gamma_D].
\tag{A.11}

The matched-coverage selective risk of arm C at c^* is

R_{\text{sel}}^C(c^*) = \mathbb{E}[\mathbb{1}[S_C = 0]],
\tag{A.12}

which is the overall error rate of arm C. This is a conservative proxy because arm C cannot abstain and must accept all trials.

