Appendix B: Tango's Score Interval: Complete Derivation and Implementation

B.1 The Paired 2×2 Table

Consider a paired experiment with N trials. Each trial i produces a pair (A_i, D_i) \in \{0,1\}^2. The cell counts are:

 D=0 D=1 Total
A=0 n_{00} n_{01} n_{0+}
A=1 n_{10} n_{11} n_{1+}
Total n_{+0} n_{+1} N

The cell probabilities are p_{ij} = \Pr(A=i, D=j), with \sum_{i,j} p_{ij} = 1. The parameter of interest is

\theta = p_{01} - p_{10}.
\tag{B.1}

B.2 Likelihood and Score Statistic

The likelihood is

L(p_{11}, p_{10}, p_{01}, p_{00}) = p_{11}^{n_{11}} p_{10}^{n_{10}} p_{01}^{n_{01}} p_{00}^{n_{00}}.
\tag{B.2}

Under the null H_0: \theta = \theta_0, we have p_{10} = p_{01} - \theta_0. Substituting into the constraint \sum p_{ij} = 1:

p_{11} = 1 - 2p_{01} + \theta_0 - p_{00}.
\tag{B.3}

The profile likelihood in p_{01} is obtained by maximizing over p_{00}. The restricted MLE \tilde p_{01} satisfies the quadratic

A \tilde p_{01}^2 + B \tilde p_{01} + C = 0,
\tag{B.4}

with coefficients

A = 2N, \quad B = -\left[ n_{10} + n_{01} + \theta_0(2N - n_{10} + n_{01}) \right], \quad C = n_{01}\theta_0(1+\theta_0).
\tag{B.5}

The positive root is

\tilde p_{01} = \frac{-B + \sqrt{B^2 - 4AC}}{2A}, \quad \tilde p_{10} = \tilde p_{01} - \theta_0.
\tag{B.6}

Remark B.1 (Sign Conventions). The coefficients in Eq. (B.5) correspond to the convention \theta = p_{01} - p_{10}. Some sources use \theta = p_{10} - p_{01}, which flips the signs of B and C. The implementation in Algorithm B.1 uses the convention \theta = p_{01} - p_{10} and the equivalent form C = -n_{01}\theta(1-\theta). These are algebraically equivalent. The canonical form should be verified against a reference implementation (e.g., the ratesci R package or Yang et al.'s non-iterative code) before deployment.

The score statistic for testing H_0: \theta = \theta_0 is

Z(\theta_0) = \frac{\hat\theta - \theta_0}{\sqrt{V(\theta_0)}},
\tag{B.7}

where \hat\theta = \hat p_{01} - \hat p_{10} = (n_{01} - n_{10})/N and

V(\theta_0) = \frac{2\tilde p_{01} - \theta_0(1+\theta_0)}{N}.
\tag{B.8}

B.3 Confidence Interval Construction

The (1-\alpha) confidence interval for \theta is

[\theta_L, \theta_U] = \{\theta_0 : |Z(\theta_0)| \le z_{\alpha/2}\}.
\tag{B.9}

The bounds are the roots of Z(\theta_0) = \pm z_{\alpha/2}, clipped at \pm 1.

Theorem B.1 (Asymptotic Coverage of Tango's Interval). Under the multinomial model with N \to \infty, the interval in Eq. (B.9) satisfies

\mathbb{P}(\theta \in [\theta_L, \theta_U]) \to 1 - \alpha.
\tag{B.10}

Proof. Under H_0: \theta = \theta_0, the score statistic Z(\theta_0) converges in distribution to \mathcal{N}(0,1) by the standard asymptotic theory of score tests for multinomial models. The interval is the set of \theta_0 for which |Z(\theta_0)| \le z_{\alpha/2}. By the duality between hypothesis tests and confidence intervals, the coverage probability converges to 1 - \alpha. \square

B.4 Non-Iterative Implementation

Algorithm B.1 (Tango's Score Interval — Non-Iterative).

```
Input: cell counts n_11, n_10, n_01, n_00, level α
Output: confidence interval [θ_L, θ_U]

1. N = n_11 + n_10 + n_01 + n_00.
2. θ̂ = (n_01 - n_10) / N.
3. z_crit = Φ^{-1}(1 - α/2).

4. Define score_statistic(θ):
   A = 2N
   B = -(n_10 + n_01) + θ(2N - n_10 + n_01)
   C = -n_01 θ (1 - θ)
   disc = max(B^2 - 4AC, 0)
   p̃_01 = (-B + sqrt(disc)) / (2A)
   p̃_10 = p̃_01 - θ
   V = (2p̃_01 - θ(1+θ)) / N
   if V ≤ 0: return 1e6 * sign(θ̂ - θ)
   return (θ̂ - θ) / sqrt(V)

5. Find θ_L: root of score_statistic(θ) = z_crit in [-0.999, θ̂].
   Fallback to -1.0 if no root.
6. Find θ_U: root of score_statistic(θ) = -z_crit in [θ̂, 0.999].
   Fallback to 1.0 if no root.
7. Return [max(θ_L, -1), min(θ_U, 1)].
```

Remark B.2 (Numerical Verification). Before deployment, Algorithm B.1 should be verified against a known reference implementation on a suite of test tables. For example, for the table (n_{11}, n_{10}, n_{01}, n_{00}) = (10, 5, 15, 70), the interval should match the output of the ratesci package or the non-iterative implementation of Yang et al. (2013). Any discrepancy indicates a coefficient error.

B.5 One-Sided Inference

For a one-sided PASS rule at level \alpha, we use

\theta_L^{1-\alpha} = \inf\{\theta_0 : Z(\theta_0) \le z_\alpha\}.
\tag{B.11}

A 95% one-sided bound requires \alpha = 0.05. The original draft conflated \alpha = 0.10 with a 95% one-sided bound; this appendix corrects the error. Throughout the primary analysis, \alpha = 0.05, giving 95% one-sided bounds.

