Appendix C: Learn-then-Test: Finite-Sample Guarantees and Fixed-Sequence Testing

C.1 The LTT Procedure

Let \Lambda = \{\gamma_1, \ldots, \gamma_K\} be candidate thresholds. For each \gamma, the selective risk is

R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma) = \frac{\sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{1}[\hat p_i \ge \gamma] \cdot \mathbb{1}[S_i = 0]}{\sum_{i=1}^n \mathbb{1}[\hat p_i \ge \gamma]}.
\tag{C.1}

Theorem C.1 (LTT Selective Risk Control). Let \delta \in (0,1) be the confidence level. For each \gamma, let \text{UCB}_\gamma be an upper confidence bound on R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma) at level 1 - \delta/K. Define

\hat\gamma = \min\{\gamma \in \Lambda : \text{UCB}_\gamma \le \alpha\},

with the convention that \hat\gamma = 1 + \epsilon (sentinel: reject-all) if no such \gamma exists. Then

\mathbb{P}\left( R_{\text{sel}}(\hat\gamma) \le \alpha \right) \ge 1 - \delta.
\tag{C.2}

Proof. For each \gamma \in \Lambda, the event \{\text{UCB}_\gamma < R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma)\} has probability at most \delta/K by the validity of the upper confidence bound. By the union bound over K hypotheses,

\mathbb{P}\left( \exists \gamma \in \Lambda : \text{UCB}_\gamma < R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma) \right) \le K \cdot \frac{\delta}{K} = \delta.

Therefore, with probability at least 1 - \delta, we have \text{UCB}_\gamma \ge R_{\text{sel}}(\gamma) for all \gamma \in \Lambda. On this event, if \hat\gamma \in \Lambda is selected, then

R_{\text{sel}}(\hat\gamma) \le \text{UCB}_{\hat\gamma} \le \alpha.

If the sentinel is selected, the risk bound is vacuous (coverage is zero), and the protocol treats this as a predictor failure. \square

C.2 Fixed-Sequence Testing

Remark C.1. Calculating many p-values would incur a multiple testing penalty, but this can be avoided using fixed-sequence testing. In fixed-sequence testing, the hypotheses are tested in a prespecified order, and testing stops at the first non-rejection. The sentinel \gamma = 1 + \epsilon is tested last. This avoids the multiplicity penalty while maintaining the finite-sample guarantee.

Corollary C.1 (Fixed-Sequence LTT). Under fixed-sequence testing with the sentinel tested last, the guarantee of Theorem C.1 holds with \delta/K replaced by \delta.

Algorithm C.1 (LTT Threshold Calibration with Fixed-Sequence Testing).

```
Input: calibration data {(p̂_i, S_i)}_{i=1}^n,
       candidate thresholds Λ = {γ_1, ..., γ_K},
       risk bound α, confidence δ
Output: threshold γ̂

1. Order thresholds: γ_1 < γ_2 < ... < γ_K.
2. Append sentinel γ_{K+1} = 1 + ε.
3. For k = 1, ..., K+1:
   a. Compute accepted mask: A_k = {i : p̂_i ≥ γ_k}.
   b. If |A_k| = 0, set UCB_k = 1.0.
   c. Else compute k_fail = Σ_{i ∈ A_k} 1[S_i = 0].
   d. Compute UCB_k = Bentkus(k_fail, |A_k|, δ).
   e. If UCB_k ≤ α, return γ_k.
4. Return γ_{K+1} = 1 + ε (sentinel: reject-all).
```

C.3 The Bentkus Upper Confidence Bound

Definition C.1 (Bentkus UCB). For k failures in n accepted trials, the Bentkus upper confidence bound on the selective risk at level 1 - \delta is

\text{UCB}_{\text{Bentkus}}(k, n, \delta) = \sup\left\{ p \in [0,1] : \mathbb{P}(\text{Bin}(n, p) \le k) \le \delta \right\}.
\tag{C.3}

The Bentkus bound is tighter than the Chernoff bound for small n and is the bound used in the LTT implementation.

Algorithm C.2 (Bentkus UCB Computation).

```
Input: k failures, n trials, confidence δ
Output: UCB p̂

1. Define g(p) = P(Bin(n, p) ≤ k) - δ.
2. Find p* such that g(p*) = 0 via bisection on [0, 1].
3. Return p*.
```

