Appendix E: Certified Fallback: Robust Invariance and Reachability

E.1 Recovery Set and Recovery Policy

Definition E.1 (Robust Control Invariant Set). A set \mathcal{R} \subseteq \mathcal{X} is robustly control invariant under policy \pi_R: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{U} if

\forall x \in \mathcal{R}, \forall d \in \mathcal{D}: \quad f(x, \pi_R(x), d) \in \mathcal{R}.
\tag{E.1}

Definition E.2 (Recovery Set and Policy). The recovery set \mathcal{R} and recovery policy \pi_R are synthesized jointly such that \mathcal{R} is robustly control invariant under \pi_R. The recovery policy is an affine feedback law \pi_R(y) = \epsilon + Ky.

E.2 Invariance and Reachability

Theorem E.1 (Certified Fallback). Let \mathcal{R} be a robust control invariant set under \pi_R. Then:

(i) Invariance. For any x_{\text{rej}} \in \mathcal{R}, the state remains in \mathcal{R} for all time under \pi_R and any disturbance sequence d \in \mathcal{D}.

(ii) Reachability. If, in addition, \mathcal{R} is constructed as a robust controllable set to a neighborhood of the goal x_g within time T_R and energy E_{\max}, then \pi_R drives x_{\text{rej}} to x_g within T_R and E_{\max}.

Proof.

(i) By Definition E.1, for all x \in \mathcal{R} and all d \in \mathcal{D}, f(x, \pi_R(x), d) \in \mathcal{R}. By induction, the state remains in \mathcal{R} for all time.

(ii) Robust controllability to x_g means that for every x \in \mathcal{R}, there exists a finite time T \le T_R and an input sequence under \pi_R such that the trajectory reaches x_g with energy E \le E_{\max}. This is an additional property that must be verified separately from invariance. In the MPT-based construction (Algorithm E.1), \mathcal{R} is computed as the maximal robust control invariant set contained in the controllable region to x_g. The verification of goal reachability is performed numerically after the invariant set is obtained. \square

Remark E.1. The original proof sketch conflated invariance with goal reachability. Invariance guarantees that the state stays inside \mathcal{R}; reachability guarantees that the goal is attained from every point in \mathcal{R}. Both properties are required for the certified fallback to be operationally meaningful.

E.3 Computation of the Recovery Set

Remark E.2. For the 2-DOF testbed, \mathcal{R} is computed using the Multi-Parametric Toolbox (MPT). The procedure:

1. Discretize the state space into a polytopic partition.
2. Compute the one-step controllable set under \pi_R and the bounded disturbance set \mathcal{D}.
3. Iterate to the fixed point to obtain the maximal robust control invariant set.
4. Verify that the goal x_g is in the interior of \mathcal{R}.
5. Verify goal reachability numerically.

Algorithm E.1 (Recovery Set Computation via MPT).

```
Input: dynamics f, recovery policy π_R, disturbance set D, state space X, goal x_g
Output: recovery set R

1. Initialize R_0 = X.
2. For k = 0, 1, 2, ...:
   a. Compute Pre(R_k) = {x ∈ R_k : ∀d ∈ D, f(x, π_R(x), d) ∈ R_k}.
   b. Set R_{k+1} = R_k ∩ Pre(R_k).
   c. If R_{k+1} = R_k, stop.
3. Return R = R_k.
4. Verify x_g ∈ int(R).
5. Verify reachability: for all x ∈ R, there exists T ≤ T_R and E ≤ E_max
   such that the trajectory under π_R reaches x_g.
```

Remark E.3 (Polytopic Representation). The sets \mathcal{R}_k are represented as polytopes in \mathbb{R}^4 (2 positions, 2 velocities). The Pre operator is computed via polytopic operations. The termination tolerance is 10^{-6}. The disturbance set \mathcal{D} is a hyperrectangle defined by the bounds on (\Delta \mathcal{I}, \Delta \mathcal{K}, \Delta \mathcal{G}) and \tau_{\text{noise}}.

Remark E.4 (Reachability Verification). After computing \mathcal{R}, goal reachability is verified numerically by simulating the closed-loop system under \pi_R from a grid of initial states in \mathcal{R} and checking that the goal is reached within T_R and E_{\max}. If any initial state fails, \mathcal{R} is restricted to the controllable subset.

