Appendix F: Conformal Prediction Under Distribution Shift

F.1 Exchangeability and Its Violation

Definition F.1 (Exchangeability). A sequence of random variables Z_1, \ldots, Z_{n+1} is exchangeable if for any permutation \pi of \{1, \ldots, n+1\},

(Z_1, \ldots, Z_{n+1}) \stackrel{d}{=} (Z_{\pi(1)}, \ldots, Z_{\pi(n+1)}).
\tag{F.1}

Theorem F.1 (Marginal Coverage). Under exchangeability of calibration and test data,

\mathbb{P}(Y \in C_\alpha(X)) \ge 1 - \alpha.
\tag{F.2}

Proof. Standard split conformal argument. See Vovk et al. (2005). \square

Remark F.1 (Violation in Robotics). In robotics, sim-to-real transfer breaks exchangeability if the real distribution contains modes absent from simulation. Standard conformal formulations rely on exchangeability assumptions that are violated by the distribution shifts inherent in real-world robotics. Existing online CP methods maintain target coverage by adaptively scaling the conformal threshold, but typically employ a static nonconformity score function. Recent work such as AdaptNC proposes joint online adaptation of both the nonconformity score parameters and the conformal threshold to reduce prediction region volume while maintaining target coverage. In the PDP protocol, Stage 2 collects a real-world calibration set and applies a fresh LTT + conformal procedure. If coverage fails, the predictor is treated as failed.

F.2 Stage 2 Calibration Protocol

Algorithm F.1 (Stage 2 Conformal Calibration).

```
Input: real-world calibration data {(X_i, Y_i)}_{i=1}^M, level α
Output: conformal quantile q̂

1. Compute nonconformity scores s_i = 1 - \hat p_{Y_i}(X_i).
2. Sort scores: s_{(1)} ≤ ... ≤ s_{(M)}.
3. Set q̂ = s_{(⌈(M+1)(1-α)⌉)}.
4. Return q̂.
```

Remark F.2 (Diagnostics). The protocol reports both marginal coverage and stratified coverage by initial-state or perturbation magnitude. If coverage fails in any stratum, the predictor is declared failed.

