Appendix I: Additional Proofs

I.1 Proof of Theorem 1 (Rejection-as-Failure Paradox)

Theorem I.1. Suppose rejection \bot counts as failure, so that for every realization \omega,

S_D(\omega) = S_C(\omega) \cdot \mathbb{1}[f(\omega) \ge \gamma].
\tag{I.1}

Then S_D(\omega) \le S_C(\omega) for all \omega, and consequently p_D \le p_C. Therefore the hypothesis H_1: p_D - p_C > \delta with \delta > 0 is logically impossible.

Proof. Since S_C(\omega) \in \{0,1\} and \mathbb{1}[f(\omega) \ge \gamma] \in \{0,1\}, the product S_C(\omega) \cdot \mathbb{1}[f(\omega) \ge \gamma] is either 0 or S_C(\omega). In both cases, S_D(\omega) \le S_C(\omega). Taking expectations over \omega,

p_D = \mathbb{E}[S_D] \le \mathbb{E}[S_C] = p_C.

Hence p_D - p_C \le 0 always. If \delta > 0, the event p_D - p_C > \delta is impossible. \square

I.2 Proof of Theorem 2 (AURC Well-Posedness)

Theorem I.2. AURC is defined only for arms that possess a confidence score function. An arm without a CSF cannot produce a risk–coverage curve, and therefore cannot have an AURC.

Proof. The risk–coverage curve requires R_{\text{sel}}(c) for all c \in [0,1]. Coverage is determined by a threshold on a CSF: c(\gamma) = \Pr[f(X) \ge \gamma]. Without a CSF, the only achievable coverages are c = 0 (reject all) and c = 1 (accept all). The integral in Eq. (A.8) requires R_{\text{sel}}(c) for all intermediate c, which is undefined. Therefore AURC is undefined for arms without a CSF. \square

I.3 Proof of Theorem 6 (Anytime-Validity of the Empirical-Bernstein CS)

Theorem I.3. The empirical-Bernstein confidence sequence in Eq. (D.1) satisfies

\mathbb{P}(\mu \in \mathcal{C}_t \text{ for all } t \ge 1) \ge 1 - \alpha.
\tag{I.2}

Proof. The proof follows from Ville's inequality applied to the nonnegative supermartingale construction in Waudby-Smith & Ramdas (2023). The sequence is a nonnegative supermartingale under the null, and Ville's inequality bounds the probability that the supermartingale ever exceeds 1/\alpha. \square

